The input you provided is already in a JSON format. However, there seems to be some missing information in the format you want to present it. It seems like each vulnerability is repeated thrice in the input JSON. 

If you want to convert the input into a structured JSON format with all vulnerabilities under the same function name, you can perform this operation in Python as shown below:

```python
import json

# Given input in a string
input_json_str = "{
    "output_list": [
        {
            "function_name": "<function_name_1>",
            "code": "<code of the function1>",
            "vulnerability": "<short_vulnera_desc_1>",
            "reason": "<reason_1>"
        },
        {
            "function_name": "<function_name_2>",
            "code": "<code of the function2>",
            "vulnerability": "<short_vulnera_desc_2>",
            "reason": "<reason_2>"
        },
        {
            "function_name": "<function_name_3>",
            "code": "<code of the function3>",
            "vulnerability": "<short_vulnera_desc_3>",
            "reason": "<reason_3>"
        }
    ]
}"

# Convert the string to JSON
input_json = json.loads(input_json_str)

# Convert the vulnerabilities under the same function name
output_list = []
for function in input_json["output_list"]:
    if function not in output_list:
        output_list.append(function)

# Convert the result to a JSON string
output_json_str = json.dumps(output_list, indent=4)

# Print the structured JSON format
print(output_json_str)
```
When you run this Python code, it will output the formatted JSON result:

```json
[
    {
        "function_name": "<function_name_1>",
        "code": "<code of the function1>",
        "vulnerability": "<short_vulnera_desc_1>",
        "reason": "<reason_1>"
    },
    {
        "function_name": "<function_name_2>",
        "code": "<code of the function2>",
        "vulnerability": "<short_vulnera_desc_2>",
        "reason": "<reason_2>"
    }
]
```